<!--
  Test graph. Wildcard over every project under `tests/`, never a hand-maintained list, so a
  newly added test project is picked up the moment it exists on disk.

  This traversal deliberately contains NOTHING from `src/`; the src projects still get built
  because each test project references them, but the traversal set itself does not leak.

  WHY THE bin/obj EXCLUDE (#2666)
  `ProjectReference` is an EXPLICIT item, so `DefaultItemExcludes` (which is what normally keeps
  `bin/` and `obj/` out of globs) does NOT apply to it. Without the exclude below MSBuild walks
  every directory under `tests/`, which on a build/test agent means thousands of `obj/` and `bin/`
  directories that the very job doing the evaluation is creating and deleting CONCURRENTLY.
  A recursive directory walk racing with that churn is how #2666 intermittently evaluated to an
  EMPTY project set on Linux CI (all 55 projects reported missing while the `leaked` assertion
  still passed - only possible with zero items), while `src/` - which is not the tree being
  churned at evaluation time - passed on the same run.

  The exclude is a strict-subset narrowing that CANNOT change the result: no `.csproj` is ever
  emitted into `bin/` or `obj/`. It only removes the racing directories from the walk.
  `TestsTraversal_BinObjExclude_DoesNotChangeTheEvaluatedSet` pins that invariant.
-->
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.Build.Traversal">

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)**\*.csproj"
                      Exclude="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)**\bin\**\*.csproj;$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)**\obj\**\*.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
